Subject: Khmer Vert - Seal Rant

Tim May responds to Bill Garland's post, and prompts the First
Extropian Squirrel to make some general comments, not specifically
directed at either:

> The issues are familiar: 

> - property rights for things that have historically not been
> considered property (air, oceans, species, etc.). Challenging.

  Yup.  There are a few precedents for separating the classically
  unrestricted owners' rights to real property into separately
  assignable subrights, notably "mineral rights" and "water rights"
  as used in parts of the United States.  It would be an interesting
  political experience to try widely to institute an additional right
  of this form, retroactively, but it might be done.  
    For example, in connection with the reintroduction of wolves in
  parts of the lower forty-eight states, there is a government program
  to compensate livestock owners for wolf kills in return for restraint
  by those owners in killing the wolves.  This program has met with
  considerable acceptance, notwithstanding obvious problems in keeping
  track of what's going on.  (See for example Bass, Rick, 1992, 
  _The_Ninemile_Wolves_, Clark City Press, Livingston, Montana.)
    Such a program could be privatized.  Thus the Squirrel can imagine
  paying for ownership of certain wolves (tagged?  radio-tracked?), or
  perhaps for the (changing) wolf content of a certain area, subject to
  paying for damage that his wolves did, but with possible income from
  tourism, photography, or, *ahem*, rodent control.  And of course,
  you'd be subject to legal process if you hurt one.

> - the "commons" probem [...] 

  Again, a real toughie, given that the original "commons" were small
  and did not wander about the landscape at will.  ("The hills are
  alive... and it's kind of frightening...")  Privatizing the oceans
  would be a thorny legal problem.  Privatizing fishing rights on the
  Grand Banks might be easier.  And how would one privatize certain
  bird or cetacean species, in which each individual animal regularly
  migrates tens of thousands of Km in a year?  Or animals like the
  snow leopard, which lives in an impenetrable, impoverished area
  where there are many hotly contested national borders?

> - can libertarians support "rationing" of a service or commodity?

  The Squirrel suspects that the trouble with rationing is that if it is
  enforced, you have a government, and libertarians will object.  But if
  it is not, then one individual, or a few, can negate its purpose.
    If that analysis is correct, then he would be quite curious whether
  there is an approach to rationing that is acceptable to libertarians.

> Perhaps Extropians could work on solutions which address the very real
> species and environmental problems ...

  Hear, hear!

>                                                  thus providing a more
> libertarian, extropic alternative to those Greens who want a return to
> a pastoral, deindustrialized world.

  I want a pastoral, deindustrialized world in the same sense that
  I want a pastoral, deindustrialized living room.  Please put the
  Bessemer converter in the garage; in here it drowns out the stero,
  blocks the view, and smudges up the curtains.  Better yet, put it
  in the asteroid belt.  And don't forget the cheap space travel to
  bring back lots of products.
    In the words of Captain Han Solo, "I can imagine a lot."  I want
  all the technical goodies you can think of, and then some, and I want
  ten million square kilometers of wild habitat for my favorite animals
  as my back yard.  I want all the constructive things technology can
  do for me and none of the destructive things it can do to me or to the
  things I care about.  What's more, I want it for a fraction of my salary.
    And how does this all come about?  Well, boundless expansion, dynamic
  optimism, intelligent technology, et cetera et cetera.  After all,
  I am an Extropian.
    ==

    Aren't you?

                              -- Jay Freeman, First Extropian Squirrel

